  "summary": {
    "en": {
      "caveats": "The study relies on a relatively small convenience sample of university students and uses self-reported online survey data, which may limit the generalizability of the causal pathways.",
      "what": "This study analyzed survey responses from 257 Indian urban youth aged 19 to 26 to evaluate how nature connectedness relates to loneliness. Using partial least squares structural equation modeling, the analysis measured the mediating roles of meaningfulness, peace of mind, and resilience, finding that the negative association between nature connection and loneliness is significantly mediated by meaningfulness and peace of mind.",
      "why": "It identifies the specific psychological pathways—meaningfulness and peace of mind, rather than resilience—through which contact with nature mitigates loneliness in young urban populations, providing a clearer mechanism for designing targeted nature-based mental health interventions."
    }
